112:# (a * b) * c cannot be evaluated when a * b is not an element, and the cube
113:# gathers by table entries, so an escaping value would index out of range.
114:# Answer 0 without touching the cube; u:is_closed is the question that matters
115:# first, and u:structure_name reports "not-a-magma".
116:def u:is_associative(t) {
125:def u:is_commutative(t) {
130:# A LATIN SQUARE: every element appears exactly once in each row and in each
131:# column. Counting is enough -- one_hot the whole table and sum along each
132:# axis, then demand every count is 1.
134:# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN. Every finite GROUP table is a Latin square:
135:# a * x = b has exactly one solution when inverses exist, so no row can repeat
136:# or omit an element. The converse is FALSE. A Latin square is exactly a
137:# QUASIGROUP -- unique solutions to a * x = b and x * a = b -- and that alone
138:# gives neither associativity nor an identity.
140:#   subtraction modulo 3   Latin square, no identity, not associative
141:#   addition modulo 3      Latin square, and a group
143:# Add an identity to a quasigroup and it is a LOOP; add associativity and it is
144:# a group. See demos/11-quasigroups.
145:def u:is_latin(t) {
153:def u:every_element_once(t, n) {
161:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
162:# Identity and inverses
163:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
165:# [n] mask: 1 at each two-sided identity.  Element e is an identity when row e
166:# reproduces the header AND column e does too.
167:def u:identity_mask(t) {
173:def u:has_identity(t) {
178:# [n, n] mask: every cell the identity touches -- its row, its column, and the
179:# corner they share, so 2n - 1 cells. Empty when there is no identity.
180:def u:mask_of_identity(t) {
189:# The identity element index, or -1 when there is none.  A monoid's identity
190:# is unique, so argmax over the mask is exact.
191:def u:identity_of(t) {
197:# [n, n] mask: 1 where a * b equals the identity, i.e. b is a right inverse of
198:# a.  Meaningless without an identity, so it is gated to the zero matrix.
199:def u:inverse_mask(t) {
205:# [n] mask: 1 at each element with a two-sided inverse.
206:def u:invertible_mask(t) {
213:def u:has_inverses(t) {
218:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
219:# The other branch: quasigroups and loops
220:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
222:# magma -> semigroup -> monoid -> group is not the only route up. Beside it:
224:#   magma  --unique division-->  quasigroup  --identity-->  loop
225:#          --associativity-->  group
227:# A QUASIGROUP is an operation where a * x = b and x * a = b always have
228:# exactly one solution -- equivalently, a Latin square. A LOOP is a quasigroup
229:# with an identity. A group is a loop that is also associative.
231:# The branch matters because the Latin pattern is easy to mistake for a group,
232:# and it is not one: subtraction modulo 3 is a quasigroup with no identity, and
233:# there are loops of order 5 that are not associative. See demos/11-quasigroups.
235:def u:is_quasigroup(t) {
240:def u:is_loop(t) {
245:# CANCELLATION. Left cancellation says a * b = a * c forces b = c, which is
246:# exactly "no element repeats within a row". Right cancellation is the same
247:# statement about columns. A finite operation with both is a quasigroup.
248:def u:left_cancels(t) {
252:def u:right_cancels(t) {
257:def u:no_repeats(t) {
267:# The first row where some element appears twice, as {found, row, value}. A
268:# repeat is a cancellation failure you can point at: two different partners
269:# give the same answer, so the equation has no unique solution.
270:def u:repeat_witness(t) {
281:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
282:# Classification -- the ladder is DERIVED, never declared
283:# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
285:# Rungs, as a record of 0/1 flags.  A structure is whatever its laws say it is.
286:def u:classify(t) {
307:# The highest rung reached, as a name.
308:def u:structure_name(t) {
